# WEBHOOKS
msgid "documentation.webhooks.title"
msgstr "Webhooks - Dulno"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.headline"
msgstr "Webhooks"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.introduction.headline"
msgstr "Introduction"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.introduction.description.1"
msgstr "Webhooks are HTTP interfaces that are used by developers and services to connect different components with each other. Dulno uses webhooks to integrate your infrastructure directly and easily into workflows. The following explains how this works in detail."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.introduction.description.2"
msgstr "We distinguish between two types of webhooks. Those that we offer you to trigger workflows and those that you provide and call up through an action according to your wishes."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.headline"
msgstr "Trigger"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.1"
msgstr "To be able to trigger a workflow via a webhook, you must first create a webhook."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.2"
msgstr "To do this, click on the “Webhooks“ tab in the sidebar. There you will find a list of all your webhooks."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.3"
msgstr "Then click on the button at the top right, which is called “create”."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.4"
msgstr "Now give the webhook a name. You can choose any name you like. Then click on add. Congratulations! You have created your first Dulno webhook."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.5"
msgstr "Click on the webhook now to see its content."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.6"
msgstr "The webhook has a URL and a key. The URL is used to call up the webhook and the key is used to authenticate you and ensure that no one else can call up the webhook."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.7"
msgstr "In the following we will show you exactly how to call the webhook and what the HTTP request must look like."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.8"
msgstr "To make the whole thing clearer, here is a concrete example. This is how our example webhook can be called up:"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.trigger.description.9"
msgstr "The payload that you attach to the request can later be used as a placeholder in the workflows."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.headline"
msgstr "Action"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.1"
msgstr "The webhooks action is used to call an external webhook and transfer data to your systems."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.2"
msgstr "Calling external endpoints with Dulno is relatively trivial compared to webhook triggers."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.3"
msgstr "The webhook specification is as follows."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.method.headline"
msgstr "HTTP Method:"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.method.description"
msgstr "The HTTP method under which the call should take place."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.url.headline"
msgstr "URL:"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.url.description"
msgstr "The URL to be called up"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.headers.headline"
msgstr "Headers:"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.headers.description"
msgstr "The headers to be attached to the request. The headers have to be in the following format Key1=Value1,Key2=Value2,..."
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.body.headline"
msgstr "Body:"
msgid "documentation.webhooks.action.description.body.description"
msgstr "The body to be included in the request"

# GLOSSARY
msgid "documentation.glossary.title"
msgstr "Glossary - Dulno"
msgid "documentation.glossary.headline"
msgstr "Glossary"
msgid "documentation.glossary.workflow.headline"
msgstr "Workflow"
msgid "documentation.glossary.workflow.description"
msgstr "Workflows are used to automate small processes within their environment. They enable the reduction and complete removal of repetitive tasks."
msgid "documentation.glossary.trigger.headline"
msgstr "Trigger"
msgid "documentation.glossary.trigger.description"
msgstr "They are the root of every workflow. This is because the triggers set the workflow in motion and initiate the chain of actions or conditions."
msgid "documentation.glossary.action.headline"
msgstr "Action"
msgid "documentation.glossary.action.description"
msgstr "Actions fulfill a task selected by you when the workflow is triggered. The order in which the actions are executed is sequential."
msgid "documentation.glossary.condition.headline"
msgstr "Condition"
msgid "documentation.glossary.condition.description"
msgstr "The conditions control the sequential process and can, for example, interrupt the further execution of actions."
msgid "documentation.glossary.template.headline"
msgstr "Template"
msgid "documentation.glossary.template.description"
msgstr "A template is a ready-made workflow. We have a wide range of templates available. So you don't have to take the time to think up new workflows on your own. Instead, you simply scroll through our templates and use one that you find useful. "
msgid "documentation.glossary.process.headline"
msgstr "Process"
msgid "documentation.glossary.process.description"
msgstr "In many cases it is an interplay of workflows that enables the most efficient automation. This is exactly what processes are used for. Each process is a sequence of so-called 'steps'."
msgid "documentation.glossary.process.step.headline"
msgstr "Process step"
msgid "documentation.glossary.process.step.description"
msgstr "A step consists of a title, a description and to-dos. Furthermore, each step can execute a workflow."
msgid "documentation.glossary.process.connection.headline"
msgstr "Process connection"
msgid "documentation.glossary.process.connection.description"
msgstr "The steps can be linked to each other via connections, allowing the execution to be controlled. To publish a workflow, there must be a connection from the start step to the end step."
msgid "documentation.glossary.database.headline"
msgstr "Database"
msgid "documentation.glossary.database.description"
msgstr "In many use cases, it can be practical to save data for or through workflows. We offer the database feature for this purpose."
msgid "documentation.glossary.webhook.headline"
msgstr "Webhook"
msgid "documentation.glossary.webhook.description"
msgstr "A webhook can be used to trigger a workflow from outside. To do this, we provide you with an HTTP endpoint that you can use to trigger it. We also offer the option of calling your own webhooks via an action. These two functionalities allow Dulno to be integrated into any project."
msgid "documentation.glossary.device.headline"
msgstr "Device"
msgid "documentation.glossary.device.description"
msgstr "For many applications, it is helpful to integrate your devices into workflows. We enable you to do this with the Dulno app. It allows you to send notifications through workflow, execute commands on your device or manage files on your devices."
msgid "documentation.glossary.organization.headline"
msgstr "Organization"
msgid "documentation.glossary.organization.description"
msgstr "An organization is a group of several Dulno users. They can automate together and thus save all members a lot of time."
msgid "documentation.glossary.account.headline"
msgstr "Account"
msgid "documentation.glossary.account.description"
msgstr "Ein Konto ist eine App oder ein Dienst, den du mit Dulno verbunden hast. Sie können diese Konten jederzeit über die Option „Konto“ entfernen."